ebral Herniation:\n  Favours first:  bruising around the eyes (raccoon eyes), bruising behind the ears (battle's sign), clear fluid leakage from nose or ears (cerebrospinal fluid leak), difficulty with balance and coordination, fatigue\n  Favours second: apathy, balance issues, breathing problems, changes in vision, coma\n\nBasilar Skull Fracture Complex (with CSF Rhinorrhoea/Hematoma) vs Diffuse Axonal Injury with Secondary Bleeding:\n  Favours first:  bruising around the eyes (raccoon eyes), bruising behind the ears (battle's sign), clear fluid leakage from nose or ears (cerebrospinal fluid leak), confusion, difficulty with balance and coordination\n  Favours second: autonomic dysfunction, behavioral changes, cognitive impairment, coma, motor dysfunction\n\nAcute Ischemic Stroke with Post-Traumatic Hemorrhagic Transformation vs Non-Traumatic Spontaneous Intracranial Hemorrhage:\n  Favours first:  deterioration in level of consciousness, dysarthria, headache, hemiplegia, hemorrhagic infarction\n  Favours second: abnormal heart rate, coma, confusion, difficulty swallowing, dizziness\n\nAcute Ischemic Stroke with Post-Traumatic Hemorrhagic Transformation vs Epidural Hematoma:\n  Favours first:  deterioration in level of consciousness, dysarthria, headache, hemiplegia, hemorrhagic infarction\n  Favours second: breathing problems, coma, confusion, dizziness, enlarged pupil in one eye\n\nAcute Ischemic Stroke with Post-Traumatic Hemorrhagic Transformation vs Subdural Hematoma with Cerebral Herniation:\n  Favours first:  deterioration in level of consciousness, dysarthria, hemiplegia, hemorrhagic infarction, increased blood pressure\n  Favours second: apathy, balance issues, breathing problems, changes in vision, coma\n\nAcute Ischemic Stroke with Post-Traumatic Hemorrhagic Transformation vs Diffuse Axonal Injury with Secondary Bleeding:\n  Favours first:  deterioration in level of consciousness, dysarthria, headache, hemiplegia, hemorrhagic infarction\n  Favours second: autonomic dysfunction, behavioral changes, cognitive impairment, coma, motor dysfunction\n\nNon-Traumatic Spontaneous Intracranial Hemorrhage vs Epidural Hematoma:\n  Favours first:  abnormal heart rate, difficulty swallowing, loss of balance or coordination, sensitivity to light, stiff neck\n  Favours second: breathing problems, enlarged pupil in one eye, severe headache, weakness on one side of the body\n\nNon-Traumatic Spontaneous Intracranial Hemorrhage vs Subdural Hematoma with Cerebral Herniation:\n  Favours first:  abnormal heart rate, loss of balance or coordination, sensitivity to light, stiff neck, sudden severe headache\n  Favours second: apathy, balance issues, breathing problems, changes in vision, difficulty walking\n\nNon-Traumatic Spontaneous Intracranial Hemorrhage vs Diffuse Axonal Injury with Secondary Bleeding:\n  Favours first:  abnormal heart rate, confusion, difficulty swallowing, dizziness, loss of balance or coordination\n  Favours second: autonomic dysfunction, behavioral changes, cognitive impairment, motor dysfunction, speech and language difficulties\n\nEpidural Hematoma vs Subdural Hematoma with Cerebral Herniation:\n  Favours first:  enlarged pupil in one eye, severe headache\n  Favours second: apathy, balance issues, changes in vision, difficulty swallowing, difficulty walking"}
