{"case_id": "MB_5", "timestep": 3, "case_summary": "A 54-year-old man is brought to the emergency department following a skiing accident. The patient lost control of his skis and collided with a tree. He lost consciousness for about 30 seconds. When he woke up, he was mildly confused but able to ski down the rest of the mountain. The patient initially denied medical treatment but his wife convinced him to be taken by ambulance to the emergency department for evaluation. The paramedics report that at the scene the patient had a Glasgow Coma Score (GCS) of 15. During the ride, he became gradually more somnolent. His medical history is significant for hypertension, hyperlipidemia, coronary artery disease, and a prior transient ischemic attack. His medications include atorvastatin and lisinopril. In the emergency department, his temperature is 97.8°F (36.6°C), blood pressure is 141/84 mmHg, pulse is 71/min, and respirations are 16/min. He is difficult to arouse with a GCS of 7. A head CT is performed and can be seen in Figure A. Which of the following is the most likely cause of this patient’s presentation?\n\nOptions:\nA. Damage to the cerebral bridging veins\nB. Damage to the sphenoid bone\nC. Occlusion of cerebral small penetrating arteries\nD. Ruptured cerebral aneurysm\nE. Secondary hemorrhage into prior area of infarction\n", "root": {"label": "Acute Post-Traumatic Altered Mental Status with Progressive GCS Decline (Brain Hematoma or Concussion) and Uncharacterised Head CT Abnormality", "time_course": "acute", "severity": "unspecified", "confidence": 0.45, "supporting_facts": ["Traumatic head injury from skiing collision", "Loss of consciousness followed by confusion then progressive somnolence", "GCS 15 at scene followed by 7 at ED (temporal progression)", "History of cerebrovascular risk factors (prior TIA, CAD, hypertension)"], "excluded_candidates": ["Subacute Cerebrovascular Event: CT would likely identify stroke, but pending ED CT findings must remain open", "Neurotoxic/metabolic encephalopathy: No evidence of toxin exposure or metabolic derangements in objective findings", "Malingering: GCS progression documented by paramedics contradicts intentional feigning of somnolence"], "alarm_features": ["Traumatic brain injury with neurological decrement", "Progressive AMS in trauma patient requiring CT", "History of stroke risk factors necessitating urgent intracranial evaluation"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Traumatic Brain Hematoma (Epidural/Subdural/Contusion-Related)", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.38888888888888884, "posterior": 0.24475524475524468, "danger": 0.85, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": ["CT findings do not show hyperdensity/mass effect patterns typical of traumatic hematomas", "Findings on CT are inconsistent with classic hematoma patterns"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3", "B1.4"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Is there localized mass effect on CT?", "Are there signs of bleeding into cerebellum or tentorium region?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Emergency head CT findings (lucid interval reversal, hyperdensity in subdural/epidural space)", "Repeat GCS measurement in ED"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism"}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Post-Traumatic Intracranial Vascular Injury and Cerebral Edema", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.22222222222222227, "posterior": 0.13986013986013987, "danger": 0.7, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": ["CT evidence of vascular abnormalities is not demonstrated, reducing support for B2", "Lack of focal deficits suggests against complex vascular injury"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B2.1", "B2.2", "B2.3"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Is there evidence of arterial dissection on imaging?", "Are there new focal neurological deficits beyond AMS?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["CT angiography for traumatic arteriovenous fistulas", "MRI to detect subtle ischaemia/hemorrhage"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.4, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy"}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Basilar Skull Fracture Complex (with CSF Rhinorrhoea/Hematoma)", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.10489510489510488, "danger": 0.65, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Absence of fracture-specific signs argues against basilar fracture"]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Acute Ischemic Stroke with Post-Traumatic Hemorrhagic Transformation", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0, "posterior": 0.05594405594405595, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": ["Imaging patterns consistent with trauma reduce concern for spontaneous bleeds", "Head CT findings (absence of lens-shaped hyperdensity for epidural hematoma, crescent-shaped hyperdensity for subdural hematoma, and parietal/occipital hemorrhage) reduce confidence in traumatic intracranial hemorrhage subtypes. No evidence of focal neurological deficits, brainstem microhemorrhages, or posterior fossa mass effect similarly lowers likelihood of traumatic vascular injury, diffuse axonal injury, or complex traumatic edema. However, absence of non-trauma-specific features (aneurysmal bleeding, hemorrhagic transformation) does not exclude non-traumatic intracranial processes."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B4.1", "B4.2"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Is there a history o