{"case_id": "MB_5", "timestep": 5, "case_summary": "A 54-year-old man is brought to the emergency department following a skiing accident. The patient lost control of his skis and collided with a tree. He lost consciousness for about 30 seconds. When he woke up, he was mildly confused but able to ski down the rest of the mountain. The patient initially denied medical treatment but his wife convinced him to be taken by ambulance to the emergency department for evaluation. The paramedics report that at the scene the patient had a Glasgow Coma Score (GCS) of 15. During the ride, he became gradually more somnolent. His medical history is significant for hypertension, hyperlipidemia, coronary artery disease, and a prior transient ischemic attack. His medications include atorvastatin and lisinopril. In the emergency department, his temperature is 97.8°F (36.6°C), blood pressure is 141/84 mmHg, pulse is 71/min, and respirations are 16/min. He is difficult to arouse with a GCS of 7. A head CT is performed and can be seen in Figure A. Which of the following is the most likely cause of this patient’s presentation?\n\nOptions:\nA. Damage to the cerebral bridging veins\nB. Damage to the sphenoid bone\nC. Occlusion of cerebral small penetrating arteries\nD. Ruptured cerebral aneurysm\nE. Secondary hemorrhage into prior area of infarction\n", "root": {"label": "Acute Post-Traumatic Altered Mental Status with Progressive GCS Decline (Brain Hematoma or Concussion) and Uncharacterised Head CT Abnormality", "time_course": "acute", "severity": "unspecified", "confidence": 0.45, "supporting_facts": ["Traumatic head injury from skiing collision", "Loss of consciousness followed by confusion then progressive somnolence", "GCS 15 at scene followed by 7 at ED (temporal progression)", "History of cerebrovascular risk factors (prior TIA, CAD, hypertension)"], "excluded_candidates": ["Subacute Cerebrovascular Event: CT would likely identify stroke, but pending ED CT findings must remain open", "Neurotoxic/metabolic encephalopathy: No evidence of toxin exposure or metabolic derangements in objective findings", "Malingering: GCS progression documented by paramedics contradicts intentional feigning of somnolence"], "alarm_features": ["Traumatic brain injury with neurological decrement", "Progressive AMS in trauma patient requiring CT", "History of stroke risk factors necessitating urgent intracranial evaluation"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Traumatic Brain Hematoma (Epidural/Subdural/Contusion-Related)", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.2095421820809685, "posterior": 0.21326226642704146, "danger": 0.85, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": ["CT findings do not show hyperdensity/mass effect patterns typical of traumatic hematomas", "Findings on CT are inconsistent with classic hematoma patterns"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3", "B1.4"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Is there localized mass effect on CT?", "Are there signs of bleeding into cerebellum or tentorium region?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Emergency head CT findings (lucid interval reversal, hyperdensity in subdural/epidural space)", "Repeat GCS measurement in ED"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism"}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Post-Traumatic Intracranial Vascular Injury and Cerebral Edema", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.1263905225250287, "posterior": 0.12863438292424728, "danger": 0.7, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": ["CT evidence of vascular abnormalities is not demonstrated, reducing support for B2", "Lack of focal deficits suggests against complex vascular injury"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B2.1", "B2.2", "B2.3"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Is there evidence of arterial dissection on imaging?", "Are there new focal neurological deficits beyond AMS?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["CT angiography for traumatic arteriovenous fistulas", "MRI to detect subtle ischaemia/hemorrhage"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.4, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy"}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Basilar Skull Fracture Complex (with CSF Rhinorrhoea/Hematoma)", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.09647578719318543, "danger": 0.65, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Absence of fracture-specific signs argues against basilar fracture"]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Acute Ischemic Stroke with Post-Traumatic Hemorrhagic Transformation", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.05423302421073958, "posterior": 0.05519584430931338, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": ["Imaging patterns consistent with trauma reduce concern for spontaneous bleeds", "Head CT findings (absence of lens-shaped hyperdensity for epidural hematoma, crescent-shaped hyperdensity for subdural hematoma, and parietal/occipital hemorrhage) reduce confidence in traumatic intracranial hemorrhage subtypes. No evidence of focal neurological deficits, brainstem microhemorrhages, or posterior fossa mass effect similarly lowers likelihood of traumatic vascular injury, diffuse axonal injury, or complex traumatic edema. However, absence of non-trauma-specific features (aneurysmal bleeding, hemorrhagic transformation) does not exclude non-traumatic intracranial processes."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B4.1", "B4.2"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Is there a history of recent ischaemic stroke?", "Would prior infarction sites on CT correlate with option E?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["CT to identify hypodensity of old infarct with hemorrhagic focus", "Stroke workup (NIHSS score, MR angiography)"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.5, "interrupt_relevance": 0.8, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "test_pathway"}, "B5": {"id": "B5", "label": "Non-Traumatic Spontaneous Intracranial Hemorrhage", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.05371597207313718, "posterior": 0.05466961274280508, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": ["Imaging patterns consistent with trauma reduce concern for spontaneous bleeds"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B5.1", "B5.2", "B5.3", "B5.4"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Is there aneurysmal pattern on CT?", "Would bleeding location align with SAH (option D) rather than trauma?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["CT with subarachnoid bleeding identification", "Lumbar puncture (if CT negative) to detect xanthochromia"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.75, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiology"}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "Epidural Hematoma", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.11847903690391191, "danger": 0.9, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "Subdural Hematoma with Cerebral Herniation", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.04739161476156477, "danger": 0.8, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["No focal mass effect or midline shift on CT reduces but does not eliminate likelihood of traumatic subdural hematoma with herniation."]}, "B1.3": {"id": "B1.3", "label": "Parietal/Occipital Cerebral Contusion with Hemorrhage", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.02961975922597798, "danger": 0.75, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B1.4": {"id": "B1.4", "label": "Diffuse Axonal Injury with Secondary Bleeding", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.01777185553558679, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B2.1": {"id": "B2.1", "label": "Traumatic Middle Cerebral Artery Dissection with Ischaemia", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.04879235214368, "danger": 0.8, "parent": "B2", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B2.2": {"id": "B2.2", "label": "Traumatic Vertebrobasilar Artery Injury with Brainstem Edema", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.03193681231222691, "danger": 0.85, "parent": "B2", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B2.3": {"id": "B2.3", "label": "Diffuse Post-Traumatic Cerebral Edema without Focal Vascular Injury", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.047905218468340366, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "B2", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B4.1": {"id": "B4.1", "label": "Secondary Hemorrhage into Prior Cerebral Infarction from Cardioembolic Mechanism (Coronary Artery Disease-Related)", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.029936729116915736, "danger": 0.85, "parent": "B4", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Failure to identify MCA territory infarct on CT reduces but does not exclude secondary hemorrhage into prior infarction, as non-focal strokes may present atypically."]}, "B4.2": {"id": "B4.2", "label": "Traumatic Cerebral Vein Injury with Post-Traumatic Hemorrhagic Stroke (e.g., Bridging Vein Laceration)", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.025259115192397645, "danger": 0.8, "parent": "B4", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B5.1": {"id": "B5.1", "label": "Ruptured Cerebral Aneurysm with Subarachnoid Hemorrhage", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.01543612595090967, "danger": 0.9, "parent": "B5", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Absence of subarachnoid hemorrhage distribution on CT makes aneurysmal bleed unlikely but paramedics must remain alert for delayed lumbar puncture requirements."]}, "B5.2": {"id": "B5.2", "label": "Hemorrhagic Cerebral Infarction from Chronic Anticoagulant Use (Lisinopril/Atorvastatin or Unknown)", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.016079297865530907, "danger": 0.75, "parent": "B5", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B5.3": {"id": "B5.3", "label": "Hypertensive Intraparenchymal Hemorrhage (Chronically Controlled)", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.01543612595090967, "danger": 0.85, "parent": "B5", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Absence of classic basal ganglia/lobar ICH in controlled hypertensive patient weakens, but does not exclude, chronic hypertensive ICH as a differential consideration."]}, "B5.4": {"id": "B5.4", "label": "Cerebral Amyloid Angiopathy with Acute Hypertensive Superimposed Hemorrhage", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.007718062975454835, "danger": 0.65, "parent": "B5", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Posterior hemorrhage patterns do not match amyloid angiopathy’s typical cortical lobar distribution, providing minor reduction in confidence for concurrent amyloid-related pathology."]}}, "frontier": [], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"timestep": 3, "bundle_id": 3, "bundle_position": 2, "bundle_size": 7, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Could the CT reveal hypodensity consistent with a prior infarct at the gray-white junction in MCA territory if a cardioembolic process is involved?", "result_summary": "Failure to identify MCA territory infarct on CT reduces but does not exclude secondary hemorrhage into prior infarction, as non-focal strokes may present atypically.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B4.1": "weak_against"}}, {"timestep": 3, "bundle_id": 3, "bundle_position": 3, "bundle_size": 7, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is high-velocity rotational impact during skiing more consistent with bridging vein laceration (B4.2) than focal traumatic hematomas?", "result_summary": "Rotational trauma patterns align with risk for cerebral vein injury but CT findings must confirm focal hyperdensity or posterior findings to strengthen this hypothesis.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B4.2": "moderate_for"}}, {"timestep": 3, "bundle_id": 3, "bundle_position": 4, "bundle_size": 7, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the CT's absence of typical subarachnoid blood distribution rule out aneurysmal bleeds, given traumatic findings as the alternative?", "result_summary": "Absence of subarachnoid hemorrhage distribution on CT makes aneurysmal bleed unlikely but paramedics must remain alert for delayed lumbar puncture requirements.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B5.1": "weak_against"}}, {"timestep": 3, "bundle_id": 3, "bundle_position": 5, "bundle_size": 7, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Would absence of intraparenchymal hemorrhage in basal ganglia/lobar regions on CT exclude chronic hypertensive ICH even with controlled BP?", "result_summary": "Absence of classic basal ganglia/lobar ICH in controlled hypertensive patient weakens, but does not exclude, chronic hypertensive ICH as a differential consideration.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B5.3": "weak_against"}}, {"timestep": 3, "bundle_id": 3, "bundle_position": 6, "bundle_size": 7, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Do the CT patterns of posterior hemorrhage (tentorative regions) align with amyloid angiopathy’s characteristic cortical lobar distribution?", "result_summary": "Posterior hemorrhage patterns do not match amyloid angiopathy’s typical cortical lobar distribution, providing minor reduction in confidence for concurrent amyloid-related pathology.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B5.4": "weak_against"}}, {"timestep": 4, "bundle_id": 4, "bundle_position": 0, "bundle_size": 1, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the lucid interval (GCS 15 at scene to 7 in ED) with neurological worsening argue for traumatic hematoma despite CT's absence of typical hyperdensity patterns?", "result_summary": "The patient's lucid interval (initial GCS 15 followed by progressive decline to GCS 7) is a classical feature of traumatic brain injury patterns such as epidural hematoma, which may progress despite atypical CT features. However, absence of CT hyperdensity/mass effect weakens definitive support for traumatic hematoma families."}], "differential_history": [{"B1": 0.24475524475524468, "B2": 0.13986013986013987, "B3": 0.10489510489510488, "B4": 0.05594405594405595, "B5": 0.06993006993006994, "B1.1": 0.1288185498711814, "B1.2": 0.0644092749355907, "B1.3": 0.03220463746779535, "B1.4": 0.019322782480677213, "B2.1": 0.053050397877984094, "B2.2": 0.034723896792862315, "B2.3": 0.05208584518929347, "B4.1": 0.0406865861411316, "B4.2": 0.01525746980292435, "B5.1": 0.02097902097902098, "B5.2": 0.017482517482517484, "B5.3": 0.02097902097902098, "B5.4": 0.01048951048951049}, {"B1": 0.2095421820809685, "B2": 0.1263905225250287, "B3": 0.09479289189377149, "B4": 0.05423302421073958, "B5": 0.05371597207313718, "B1.1": 0.11641232337831584, "B1.2": 0.04656492935132634, "B1.3": 0.02910308084457896, "B1.4": 0.01746184850674738, "B2.1": 0.047941232681907434, "B2.2": 0.031379715937248503, "B2.3": 0.04706957390587275, "B4.1": 0.02941452160582486, "B4.2": 0.02481850260491472, "B5.1": 0.01516686270300344, "B5.2": 0.015798815315628583, "B5.3": 0.01516686270300344, "B5.4": 0.00758343135150172}, {"B1": 0.21326226642704146, "B2": 0.12863438292424728, 